<TopicPage slug="subjects/media-studies/foundational/the-theoretical-framework">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Media Studies is not watching television with opinions. The WJEC Eduqas GCSE is built on a
    <strong>theoretical framework</strong> — a fixed set of four questions you can aim at any
    media product, from a 1960s newspaper front page to this morning's TikTok. Every product in
    every one of the nine forms the specification studies (advertising and marketing, film,
    magazines, music video, newspapers, radio, television, video games, and online, social and
    participatory media) is analysed through the same lens. Learn the four areas and you have
    learned the grammar of the entire subject.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the framework, one area at a time — each area comes with its own guiding question" widget="equationStep" config={fwCfg} />

  <h2>Four questions, one product</h2>
  <p>
    The framework works because the areas are <em>inter-related</em>: the specification's word,
    and the difference between analysis and a checklist. Take one music video. Its
    <strong>media language</strong> — performance footage intercut with narrative, the star's
    wardrobe, the edit's pace — constructs meaning from codes you can name. Those codes carry its
    <strong>representations</strong>: of gender, wealth, place, who gets to be the hero. But the
    video exists because a <strong>media industry</strong> paid for it to promote a track, and
    the industry's platform — a streaming service's algorithm, a social feed — decides its reach.
    And its <strong>audience</strong> is not passive: viewers stream, skip, remix, comment and
    re-upload, which changes what the industry funds next. One product, four areas, all
    touching. That loop is the subject.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading through contexts</h2>
  <p>
    Alongside the framework, the specification requires three <strong>contexts</strong> — the
    circumstances a product is made in and reflects. <strong>Historical context</strong>: how the
    product shows the time it was made, in its language, genre conventions, representations and
    in how it was produced, distributed and consumed (a magazine sold at a newsstand belongs to a
    different media world from one distributed as an app). <strong>Social and cultural
    context</strong>: how the product reflects the society around it, the genres, styles and
    technologies it borrows — and how audience responses to it change over time, so a
    representation that read as harmless in one decade can read as damaging in the next.
    <strong>Political context</strong>: the politics inside a product's viewpoints and
    representations, and the politics around it — ownership, regulation, censorship. The habit
    to build is asking of any product: <em>when</em> is this from, <em>what society</em> is it
    speaking to, and <em>whose interests</em> does it serve?
  </p>

  <h2>The audiences area, measured</h2>
  <p>
    Of the four areas, audiences changed fastest in the framework's lifetime. The curve below is
    the number of people using social media worldwide — from under a billion in 2010 to more
    than five billion by 2024. It is the clearest single picture of why "audiences" now includes
    production: five billion people hold a channel to other audiences in their pockets, which is
    precisely how the syllabus phrases it — audience members "become producers themselves".
    It also reshaped the industries area, as distribution and advertising money followed the
    curve.
  </p>
  <p>
    Notice, too, what the curve implies about targeting — the first verb in the audiences area's
    own definition. An audience of five billion is really millions of overlapping micro-audiences:
    the same platforms that deliver a mass audience can address a sixteen-year-old skater in
    one city and a retiree in another with different feeds, advertising and even different news.
    When you analyse how a product "targets, reaches and addresses" its audience, you are being
    asked to name who it imagines watching, how it knows where to find them, and what tone it
    uses when it gets there — three questions that barely existed when audiences were measured
    by circulation figures and programme schedules.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="global social media users, 2010–2024 — the audiences area rewriting itself in real time" widget="plotter" config={smCfg} />

  <p>
    Hold framework and contexts together and you have the discipline in miniature: name the
    codes, interrogate the representations, trace the money and the platform, and never forget
    that the audience — now more than five billion of them with upload buttons — is part of the
    text, not outside it. That is the work every component of the GCSE assesses, and it starts
    with these four questions.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> WJEC Eduqas GCSE Media Studies specification, "Theoretical framework" and "Contexts of media" (components 1–2); DataReportal / We Are Social, <em>Digital</em> global reports (2010–2024); S. Hall, "Encoding/Decoding" (1980).</p>
</TopicPage>
